window.auguraToolData.ogham = {"aicmi":[{"name":"Aicme Beithe","note":"strokes to one side of the stem, 1 to 5"},{"name":"Aicme Úatha","note":"strokes to the other side of the stem, 1 to 5"},{"name":"Aicme Muine","note":"slanting strokes across the stem, 1 to 5"},{"name":"Aicme Ailme","note":"short notches through the stem, 1 to 5 (the vowels)"}],"feda":[{"name":"Beith","tree":"Birch","tree_irish":"beith","aicme":0,"strokes":[[0.5,0.85,0.85,0.85]],"kennings":[{"irish":"maise malach","english":"beauty of the eyebrow"}],"keywords":["beginnings","cleansing","first steps"],"meaning":"Birch is the first tree to green a bare hillside, so Beith stands for a fresh start. Sweep out what is finished and begin small; the ground is ready.","lore":"Birch is a pioneer tree that seeds itself on cleared and burnt land, and its pale bark shines in winter. It is the first letter of the ogham, one stroke beside the stem."},{"name":"Luis","tree":"Rowan","tree_irish":"caorthann","aicme":0,"strokes":[[0.5,0.85,0.85,0.85],[0.5,0.7,0.85,0.7]],"kennings":[{"irish":"lí súla","english":"delight of the eye"}],"keywords":["protection","watchfulness","bright berries"],"meaning":"Rowan guards the doorway. Trust your first sense of what is safe, and put a quiet boundary around what matters to you.","lore":"Its red berries ripen in late summer and the tree was planted near houses in Ireland and Scotland to keep harm away."},{"name":"Fearn","tree":"Alder","tree_irish":"fearnóg","aicme":0,"strokes":[[0.5,0.85,0.85,0.85],[0.5,0.7,0.85,0.7],[0.5,0.55,0.85,0.55]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["resilience","crossing water","steady ground"],"meaning":"Alder keeps its feet in the wet and does not rot. You can stand firm in a muddy situation; build your footing before you move on.","lore":"Alder grows along streams and its wood turns a deep red when cut. Because it lasts under water, it was used for piles, bridges and bowls."},{"name":"Sail","tree":"Willow","tree_irish":"saileach","aicme":0,"strokes":[[0.5,0.85,0.85,0.85],[0.5,0.7,0.85,0.7],[0.5,0.55,0.85,0.55],[0.5,0.4,0.85,0.4]],"kennings":[{"irish":"lí ngrúade","english":"colour of the cheek"}],"keywords":["flexibility","feeling","tides"],"meaning":"Willow bends and does not break. Let your feelings move like water, and give way where holding stiff would only hurt.","lore":"Willow loves damp ground, roots from a cut twig and was woven into baskets and fences. Its bark was chewed for aches."},{"name":"Nion","tree":"Ash","tree_irish":"fuinseog","aicme":0,"strokes":[[0.5,0.85,0.85,0.85],[0.5,0.7,0.85,0.7],[0.5,0.55,0.85,0.55],[0.5,0.4,0.85,0.4],[0.5,0.25,0.85,0.25]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["connection","reach","tools"],"meaning":"Ash is tall, straight and useful. Link what is above with what is below: a plan with a first action, a wish with a tool.","lore":"Ash wood is strong and springy, so it made spear shafts, oars, hurleys and handles. Old stories give it a place between the worlds."},{"name":"Úath","tree":"Hawthorn","tree_irish":"sceach gheal","aicme":1,"strokes":[[0.15,0.85,0.5,0.85]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["restraint","thresholds","hidden gifts"],"meaning":"Hawthorn has thorns and white blossom together. Wait before you act; something sharp guards something lovely.","lore":"Lone hawthorns on fields were left standing out of respect, and its blossom in May marked the turn to summer. The name Úath is linked with fear or terror."},{"name":"Dair","tree":"Oak","tree_irish":"dair","aicme":1,"strokes":[[0.15,0.85,0.5,0.85],[0.15,0.7,0.5,0.7]],"kennings":[{"irish":"ardam fedo","english":"highest of woods"}],"keywords":["strength","doorways","patience"],"meaning":"Oak is slow, sturdy and long-lived. What you build now will last if you give it time; stay with it.","lore":"The word dair is the root of the Irish word for door (doras), and oak was the timber of halls and ships. Some old oaks are many centuries old."},{"name":"Tinne","tree":"Holly","tree_irish":"cuileann","aicme":1,"strokes":[[0.15,0.85,0.5,0.85],[0.15,0.7,0.5,0.7],[0.15,0.55,0.5,0.55]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["courage","defense","evergreen"],"meaning":"Holly stays green through winter and wears its spikes. Be brave and keep your edges; some things deserve a clear no.","lore":"Holly leaves were fed to livestock in lean winters and its hard, pale wood was carved and turned. Cut branches were brought indoors at midwinter."},{"name":"Coll","tree":"Hazel","tree_irish":"coll","aicme":1,"strokes":[[0.15,0.85,0.5,0.85],[0.15,0.7,0.5,0.7],[0.15,0.55,0.5,0.55],[0.15,0.4,0.5,0.4]],"kennings":[{"irish":"caínem fedo","english":"fairest of trees"},{"irish":"millsem fedo","english":"sweetest of woods"}],"keywords":["wisdom","learning","small rewards"],"meaning":"Hazel gives nuts to those who wait for autumn. Knowledge comes by patient gathering, so read, ask and let it ripen.","lore":"In Irish tales the nuts of hazels by a sacred well feed the salmon of wisdom. Hazel rods were also used to find water and to make fences."},{"name":"Ceirt","tree":"Apple","tree_irish":"ull","aicme":1,"strokes":[[0.15,0.85,0.5,0.85],[0.15,0.7,0.5,0.7],[0.15,0.55,0.5,0.55],[0.15,0.4,0.5,0.4],[0.15,0.25,0.5,0.25]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["choice","sweetness","offering"],"meaning":"Apple offers fruit and asks you to choose. Pick what you truly want, enjoy it, and share the rest.","lore":"The tree for this letter is debated; some scholars read ceirt as apple, others as a wild fruit tree or quince. We show apple, the best-loved reading."},{"name":"Muin","tree":"Vine","tree_irish":"fíniúin","aicme":2,"strokes":[[0.15,0.91,0.85,0.79]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["gathering","mood","harvest"],"meaning":"The vine ripens slowly and is gathered all at once. Notice what has grown ripe in you, and be honest about your mood.","lore":"Some sources give bramble instead of vine for this letter. Both climb, twist and bear fruit that stains the hands."},{"name":"Gort","tree":"Ivy","tree_irish":"eidhneán","aicme":2,"strokes":[[0.15,0.91,0.85,0.79],[0.15,0.76,0.85,0.64]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["persistence","spirals","holding on"],"meaning":"Ivy climbs a little at a time and wraps what it meets. Keep going in small turns, and ask if something is holding you or you are holding it.","lore":"Ivy flowers late and feeds bees in autumn; it stays green all winter. The name gort also means a field or garden."},{"name":"Ngéadal","tree":"Reed","tree_irish":"giolcach","aicme":2,"strokes":[[0.15,0.91,0.85,0.79],[0.15,0.76,0.85,0.64],[0.15,0.61,0.85,0.49]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["voice","healing","straight speech"],"meaning":"A reed is hollow, and wind turns it into a note. Say the simple true thing, and let it carry.","lore":"Reeds thatched roofs and made pipes and pens. The sound ng is rare at the start of English words, but common in Irish."},{"name":"Straif","tree":"Blackthorn","tree_irish":"draighean","aicme":2,"strokes":[[0.15,0.91,0.85,0.79],[0.15,0.76,0.85,0.64],[0.15,0.61,0.85,0.49],[0.15,0.46,0.85,0.34]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["hardship","sharp truth","strength through trouble"],"meaning":"Blackthorn blooms on bare black branches in the cold. A hard stretch is real, but it carries its own early flower.","lore":"Blackthorn gives sloes after the first frosts and its wood makes stout walking sticks. The letter's sound is unclear and scholars argue about it."},{"name":"Ruis","tree":"Elder","tree_irish":"tromán","aicme":2,"strokes":[[0.15,0.91,0.85,0.79],[0.15,0.76,0.85,0.64],[0.15,0.61,0.85,0.49],[0.15,0.46,0.85,0.34],[0.15,0.31,0.85,0.19]],"kennings":[{"irish":"tindem ruccae","english":"intensest of blushes"}],"keywords":["endings","renewal","ripeness"],"meaning":"Elder blooms and then fruits and fades fast. Let something finish, so the next thing can take its place.","lore":"The name is tied to the word for red, and to blushing. Elder flowers and berries are used for cordial and wine, and the tree grows near old homes."},{"name":"Ailm","tree":"Silver fir","tree_irish":"giúis","aicme":3,"strokes":[[0.38,0.85,0.62,0.85]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["clear sight","height","long view"],"meaning":"Fir stands straight and sees far. Step back and take the long view before you decide.","lore":"Ailm is read as fir or pine, a tall evergreen with a resinous scent. It is also thought to relate to the cry of pain, and has the first vowel's sound."},{"name":"Onn","tree":"Gorse","tree_irish":"aiteann","aicme":3,"strokes":[[0.38,0.85,0.62,0.85],[0.38,0.7,0.62,0.7]],"kennings":[{"irish":"congnamh ech","english":"helper of horses"}],"keywords":["warmth","gold","enduring cheer"],"meaning":"Gorse flowers gold even in hard weather. Be generous with warmth, and expect good cheer to come back to you.","lore":"Gorse is prickly but nearly always in flower, and it smells of coconut on warm days. It was cut for fodder, fuel and kindling."},{"name":"Úr","tree":"Heather","tree_irish":"fraoch","aicme":3,"strokes":[[0.38,0.85,0.62,0.85],[0.38,0.7,0.62,0.7],[0.38,0.55,0.62,0.55]],"kennings":[],"keywords":["home","open ground","belonging"],"meaning":"Heather covers open moor and feeds the bees. Return to simple ground; belonging is a bigger gift than status.","lore":"The word úr can mean fresh or soil, and some lists give heather, others clay or earth. Heather was used for bedding, brooms and ale."},{"name":"Edad","tree":"Aspen","tree_irish":"crann creathach","aicme":3,"strokes":[[0.38,0.85,0.62,0.85],[0.38,0.7,0.62,0.7],[0.38,0.55,0.62,0.55],[0.38,0.4,0.62,0.4]],"kennings":[{"irish":"commaín carat","english":"exchange of friends"}],"keywords":["listening","trembling","exchange"],"meaning":"Aspen leaves tremble at the faintest breeze. Listen, answer in kind, and do not mistake a shiver for fear alone.","lore":"The Irish name means trembling tree. Aspen spreads by roots into whole groves that share one origin, a quiet picture of friends."},{"name":"Idad","tree":"Yew","tree_irish":"iúr","aicme":3,"strokes":[[0.38,0.85,0.62,0.85],[0.38,0.7,0.62,0.7],[0.38,0.55,0.62,0.55],[0.38,0.4,0.62,0.4],[0.38,0.25,0.62,0.25]],"kennings":[{"irish":"sinem fedo","english":"oldest of woods"}],"keywords":["endurance","memory","the long cycle"],"meaning":"Yew can outlive empires and regrow from its own hollow heart. Honor what is old in you, and trust that endings turn into beginnings.","lore":"Yews grow for a thousand years or more and often stand in old churchyards. Every part of the tree except the red fleshy aril is poisonous."}]};
